.TH PIPE_PROGRESS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
pipe_progress \- pass data through a pipe, showing activity with dots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pipe_progress
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pipe_progress
copies standard input to standard output unchanged, and writes dots to
standard error, so that a long pipeline shows that data is still
flowing.
It takes no file operands: it reads standard input until end of input.
.PP
It reads in blocks of at most 4096 bytes.
After each read, it takes the time from the system clock, in whole
seconds.
If that differs from the time at start-up, or from the time of the
previous dot, it writes a dot to standard error and then writes the
block to standard output.
Each dot is written to standard error at once.
.PP
So a dot is written at most once per block, and a steady stream gives
about one dot per second.
The first dot can come less than a second after the start.
Dots mark arriving data, not elapsed time: nothing is written while a
read waits, and the first block after a stall of any length gets one
dot.
A pipeline whose data all arrives within the clock second in which it
started writes no dots.
There is no total or percentage; the program does not know how much
data to expect.
.PP
At end of input,
.B pipe_progress
writes a newline to standard error, even when it wrote no dots, so that
the next prompt starts on a fresh line.
.PP
An error reading standard input ends the copy as if at end of input.
An error writing standard output is ignored, and the rest of that block
is lost, unless standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
Neither is reported.
.SH OPTIONS
No option is examined.
Any argument other than a first argument of
.B \-\-help
is ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
This includes the case where standard input could not be read or
standard output could not be written, other than as below.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output or standard error is a pipe whose reader has exited.
This is noticed only when a write to it fails: on standard output when
the next block arrives, and on standard error at the next dot or the
final newline, even after all the data was copied.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show activity while a directory tree is archived and compressed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar cf - src | pipe_progress | bzip2 >src.tar.bz2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a large file to another drive, with dots on the console:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ pipe_progress <disk.img >d:/disk.img
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The dots go to standard error, so they still show when standard output
is redirected to a file or another command.
Redirect standard error to hide them.
.PP
The time comes from the system clock, not from a monotonic timer.
If the clock is set back, the next block also gets a dot.
.PP
Standard input, output, and error are binary: CR LF pairs, NUL bytes,
and Ctrl-Z characters in the data pass through unchanged.
.PP
Lines typed at a console arrive with CR LF line endings.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
Elsewhere in a line, Ctrl-Z is copied as data, and Ctrl-D is an ordinary
character.
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later, non-ASCII characters typed at a
console arrive in the console's input code page, not in UTF-8.
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
Where busybox emulates them, an escape sequence split between two blocks
is not recognized, and is written as text.
A block that contains a NUL byte is written as is, with no conversion
or emulation.
This is decided for each block, so other blocks are still converted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR bzip2 (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR tar (1)
